 "issue": 1689,
 "issue_title": "Bug: validate writer output path arguments",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n`write_csv()` and `write_parquet()` convert their output path with `os.fspath(path)` before validating the public path argument. Invalid path values such as `True` or arbitrary objects currently surface Python's low-level `os.fspath()` error instead of an Arnio-specific writer error.\n\n## Why it matters\nWriter APIs are commonly used in notebooks, scripts, and CI jobs. A clear validation message helps users distinguish invalid output-path input from writer/backend failures.\n\n## Evidence\n- `arnio/io.py`: `write_csv()` calls `path = os.fspath(path)` before any path-type validation.\n- `arnio/io.py`: `write_parquet()` does the same before extension/compression validation.\n- Existing issue #1408 covers invalid `frame` inputs for writers, but not invalid writer output paths.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n1. Run:\n   ```python\n   import arnio as ar\n   import pandas as pd\n\n   frame = ar.from_pandas(pd.DataFrame({\"a\": [1]}))\n   ar.write_csv(frame, True)\n   ar.write_parquet(frame, True)\n   ```\n2. Observe raw `TypeError: expected str, bytes or os.PathLike object, not bool` from `os.fspath()`.\n\n## Expected behavior\nWriters should reject invalid output paths with a clear public API error, for example `TypeError: path must be a string, bytes, or os.PathLike object`.\n\n## Actual behavior\nThe raw `os.fspath()` error leaks through before Arnio can explain which argument is invalid.\n\n## Suggested direction\n- Add a small path validator for writer output paths.\n- Explicitly reject booleans.\n- Keep valid `str`, `bytes`, and `os.PathLike` behavior unchanged.\n- Add tests for invalid `path` values in `tests/test_write_csv.py` and `tests/test_parquet.py`.\n\n## References\n- `arnio/io.py`\n- `tests/test_write_csv.py`\n- `tests/test_parquet.py`\n- Related issue: #1408 covers writer `frame` validation.\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1689",
 "pr": 1860,
 "pr_title": "fix: validate writer output path before os.fspath in write_csv and write_parquet",
 "pr_body": "\u2026arquet\r\n\r\n## Summary\r\nAdded a type check before calling `os.fspath(path)` in both `write_csv()` and `write_parquet()`. Earlier, passing values like `bool` or `int` caused an unclear error from `os.fspath()`. With this change, invalid path inputs now raise a more understandable `TypeError` that clearly points to the `path` argument.\r\n\r\n<!-- What changed and why? Keep this short but specific. -->\r\n\r\n<!-- Need quick setup or contribution help? Join Discord: https://discord.gg/xsEw7r78M -->\r\n\r\n## Linked issue\r\nFixes #1689\r\n\r\n<!-- Use \"Fixes #123\" when the PR fully resolves an issue. Use \"Refs #123\" for partial work. -->\r\n\r\n## Type of change\r\n- [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] Feature\r\n- [ ] Performance improvement\r\n- [ ] Documentation\r\n- [x] Tests\r\n- [ ] Refactor\r\n- [ ] CI / packaging\r\n\r\n\r\n## Area\r\n- [ ] CSV parser / I/O\r\n- [ ] C++ cleaning engine\r\n- [x] Python API / ArFrame\r\n- [ ] Pipeline / custom steps\r\n- [ ] Data quality / profiling\r\n- [ ] Schema validation\r\n- [ ] pandas interoperability\r\n- [ ] Docs / website\r\n- [ ] Developer tooling\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n<!-- Paste commands you ran and summarize the result. -->\r\n- [x] `python -m pytest tests/test_write_csv.py tests/test_parquet.py -v` \u2014 4 new tests pass\r\n- [ ] `make test` (or `python -m pytest tests -v --cov=arnio`)\r\n- [ ] `make lint` (or run separately:\r\n  ```powershell\r\n  python -m ruff check .\r\n  python -m black --check .\r\n  ```\r\n)\r\n- [ ] optionally `python -m pytest tests -v --tb=short -x`\r\n- [ ] Other:\r\n\r\n## Screenshots / Media\r\nN/A\r\n<!-- If this PR changes the UI, README visuals, or terminal output, add a screenshot or GIF. -->\r\n\r\n## Performance Impact\r\nNone \u2014 validation runs before any I/O.\r\n<!-- If this PR affects speed or memory, paste a snippet from `make benchmark` or other tools. -->\r\n\r\n## GSSoC labels\r\n\r\n<!-- Maintainers only: use exact scoring labels where applicable, for example `gssoc:approved`, `level:beginner`, `quality:clean`, and `type:bug`. Do not add labels only to increase points. -->\r\n\r\n## Contributor checklist\r\n- [x] I read the contributing guide.\r\n- [x] I kept the PR focused on one issue or one logical change.\r\n- [x] I added or updated tests for behavior changes.\r\n- [ ] I added or updated docs/examples for public API changes.\r\n- [x] I checked that generated files, logs, and local build artifacts are not committed.\r\n- [x] My PR title uses Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`).\r\n\r\n## Maintainer notes\r\n<!-- Optional: risks, migration notes, release notes, or follow-up work. -->\r\n",
